      "title": "Adjunct Assistant Professor of Theology",
      "description": "Posted: 2026-10-30\n\nLocation: Crestview Hills\n\nAbout the Role: The Adjunct Assistant Professor of Theology plays a pivotal role in delivering high-quality theological education to undergraduate students within an academic institution. This position is responsible for teaching courses that explore various dimensions of theology, including historical, systematic, and practical aspects, fostering critical thinking and spiritual growth. The role involves engaging students through dynamic lectures, discussions, and mentorship, contributing to their academic and personal development. Additionally, the professor collaborates with faculty colleagues to enhance the curriculum and participates in departmental activities that support the institution's mission. Ultimately, this position aims to cultivate a deep understanding of theological concepts and their application in contemporary contexts, preparing students for diverse careers and vocations. Minimum Qualifications: Master’s degree in Theology, Religious Studies, Divinity, or a closely related field from an accredited institution. Demonstrated expertise in theological scholarship and teaching experience at the college or university level. Strong communication and interpersonal skills to effectively engage with a diverse student body. Ability to prepare and deliver course materials in both in-person and online formats. Commitment to fostering an inclusive and respectful learning environment. Preferred Qualifications: Doctoral degree (Ph.D. or equivalent) in Theology or a related discipline. Experience with curriculum development and academic program assessment. Familiarity with contemporary theological debates and interdisciplinary approaches. Proficiency in digital teaching tools and learning management systems. Active participation in scholarly research, publications, or professional theological organizations. Responsibilities: Develop and deliver course content in theology that aligns with academic standards and institutional goals. Facilitate engaging classroom discussions and provide constructive feedback to support student learning and critical inquiry. Assess student performance through assignments, exams, and projects, ensuring fair and consistent grading practices. Advise and mentor students on academic and career pathways related to theology and religious studies. Collaborate with department faculty to review and update curriculum, participate in faculty meetings, and contribute to program development. Skills: The required and preferred skills are integral to the daily responsibilities of the Adjunct Assistant Professor of Theology. Strong communication skills enable the professor to clearly convey complex theological concepts and engage students in meaningful dialogue. Expertise in curriculum development and assessment ensures that course content remains relevant and rigorous, meeting academic standards. Proficiency with digital tools supports the delivery of courses in various formats, enhancing accessibility and student interaction. Additionally, research and scholarly skills contribute to the professor’s ability to stay current in the field and bring fresh perspectives into the classroom, enriching the educational experience.",
